INSERT INTO drafts ("id", "window_id", "cluster_id", "channel", "prompt_version", "content", "content_hash", "source_event_ids", "status", "reviewed_by", "review_notes", "approved_at", "created_at", "updated_at", "guardrail_score", "guardrail_passed", "guardrail_reasons", "llm_model", "reviewer_id", "reviewer_note", "reviewed_at", "generated_at") VALUES ('afb65ab86b715b5a9a8c4d99b8278e3d'::uuid, '20260623T1355_20261005T1755', '20260623T1355_20261005T1755_c0', 'LINKEDIN', 'v2', 'When a marketplace experiment breaches its error‑budget, the first line of defense is granular error capture. In a recent Postgres‑backed cron worker run, a Node.js background job runner struggled to separate treatment and control tenants in the on‑call view. A similar challenge appears in uptime monitoring for a startup’s Node.js checkout edge: the healthcheck must be GDPR‑aware and link alerts to revenue paths without duplicating customer data. On 3 October 2026, a builder showcased an AI buyer agent that paid only the honest seller, with a 0.05 USDC escrow transfer and a 0.95 USDC back to the buyer, verified on BaseScan.

What changed:
- Capture a single immutable event per retry, keyed by a stable job ID and cohort label, and emit the terminal outcome as a separate event. (Postgres cron worker)
- Deploy an external probe for the public checkout endpoint, a separate heartbeat for scheduled payment‑reconciliation, and an internal metric for completed checkout attempts. (Node.js uptime API)
- Publish status from a minimal set of service‑level states and tag every signal with a stable service and cost‑center label, but exclude order IDs and raw request bodies. (Node.js uptime API)

