INSERT INTO drafts ("id", "window_id", "cluster_id", "channel", "prompt_version", "content", "content_hash", "source_event_ids", "status", "reviewed_by", "review_notes", "approved_at", "created_at", "updated_at", "guardrail_score", "guardrail_passed", "guardrail_reasons", "llm_model", "reviewer_id", "reviewer_note", "reviewed_at", "generated_at") VALUES ('e675682c66ad5b5eaffc9561b1e7aaef'::uuid, '20260623T1400_20261005T1800', '20260623T1400_20261005T1800_c1', 'LINKEDIN', 'v2', 'Need to expose a local service without a public IP or VPN?  
When you run a development server or a home device behind NAT, you often need external access without a public IP or router configuration; SSH reverse tunnels (ssh -R) let a listening port on a remote server forward traffic back to your machine, keeping the connection encrypted.  
The listener opens on the remote side, and everything that hits the remote port is piped through the SSH channel to your laptop, which is useful for catching webhooks, exposing local APIs, or debugging services that otherwise cannot be reached.  
This technique eliminates the need for a full VPN setup, reduces latency compared to tunneling through a proxy, and leverages the security and authentication mechanisms already built into OpenSSH, which by default listens on port 22.  

- ssh -R 0.0.0.0:8080:localhost:80 exposes local port 80 through remote port 8080.  
- Enable GatewayPorts to allow connections from any remote host, not just the SSH client.  
- If you use a non‑standard SSH port, specify -p 2222 to avoid conflicts.  

SSH reverse tunnels provide a lightweight, secure alternative to VPNs for exposing internal services without a public IP.  

How often do you use reverse tunnels in your workflow, and what challenges have you encountered?', '2200e7ce9eddcab05a0224dbc64e00660686371c3aebb680c9ee9d05ea5f4d9a', '{9a5d100a21e947dab78a90eed77b992e,d45ac8deb03b4713a75c61abd5fecd9d,cd7dc6fe62424bdebc2827c107bf7bd5}'::uuid[]::uuid[], 'IN_REVIEW', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2026-10-05 17:58:47.532764+00:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-05 18:00:35.412103+00:00'::timestamptz, 1.0, true, '{}'::jsonb, 'openai/gpt-oss-20b', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2026-10-05 18:00:35.390219+00:00'::timestamptz) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO drafts ("id", "window_id", "cluster_id", "channel", "prompt_version", "content", "content_hash", "source_event_ids", "status", "reviewed_by", "review_notes", "approved_at", "created_at", "updated_at", "guardrail_score", "guardrail_passed", "guardrail_reasons", "llm_model", "reviewer_id", "reviewer_note", "reviewed_at", "generated_at") VALUES ('17061ab41de75c98aada9557bbec0d34'::uuid, '20260623T1359_20261005T1759', '20260623T1359_20261005T1759_c0', 'LINKEDIN', 'v2', 'Managing multiple AI agents across projects can be chaotic without a unified workflow.  
Developers often open separate terminals for Codex, Gemini CLI, and Claude, each with its own permission prompts and audit trail. Bellows, a free desktop client for Windows, Mac, and Linux, consolidates these agents into a single workspace, enforcing one set of rules and recording permissions uniformly. It is an Agent Client Protocol (ACP) client, so any ACP‑compatible agent—Codex, Gemini CLI, Qwen Code, or a local Ollama or LM Studio model—can be launched with one click. Permission prompts become consistent buttons across agents, simplifying compliance and traceability.

- Bellows runs Codex, Gemini CLI, and Claude side‑by‑side in separate tabs within the same project.  
- It includes presets for the three agents and supports local model sessions via Ollama or LM Studio with a single click.  
- Permission prompts are rendered as identical buttons, creating a single audit trail for all agents.

Adopting Bellows also streamlines tool discovery and local model integration. With a single click, developers can launch a local Ollama or LM Studio session, eliminating the need to open separate terminals or manage environment variables.  
This approach aligns with best practices for secure, auditable AI development.  
In environments where compliance is critical, a unified permission framework reduces the risk of accidental data exposure.  

