INSERT INTO drafts ("id", "window_id", "cluster_id", "channel", "prompt_version", "content", "content_hash", "source_event_ids", "status", "reviewed_by", "review_notes", "approved_at", "created_at", "updated_at", "guardrail_score", "guardrail_passed", "guardrail_reasons", "llm_model", "reviewer_id", "reviewer_note", "reviewed_at", "generated_at") VALUES ('2c829319796a5f2cb7cd021f3da8af3a'::uuid, '20260623T1415_20261005T1815', '20260623T1415_20261005T1815_c0', 'LINKEDIN', 'v2', 'When a single idempotent polling worker can reduce alert noise by 90%, teams finally stop drowning in false positives.  
In a media SaaS that processes millions of AI agent calls per day, every failed tool call, slow model turn, or retry can trigger an incident. Traditional alerting systems treat each failure as a separate event, overwhelming on‑call engineers and eroding trust in monitoring. A recent case study shows that by instrumenting each AI run as one auditable operation, keeping metrics low‑cardinality, and attaching request_id, trace_id, region, model usage, and attempt data to structured logs, the team could batch failures into 5‑minute windows. The idempotent polling worker evaluated the window, wrote a durable decision record, and emitted a single alert per window. This approach preserved exactly‑once alert semantics while keeping the underlying polling at‑least‑once. The system now reports a 90% reduction in alert noise while maintaining 99.9% detection of true outages.  
What changed:  
• Low‑cardinality metrics and structured logs replaced per‑failure counters.  
• A durable decision record ensured once‑only notifications, even with at‑least‑once delivery.  
• The system now reports a 90% reduction in alert noise while maintaining 99.9% detection of true outages.  
• The streaming endpoint in Next.js 15 uses gpt‑4o‑mini via Vercel AI SDK.  
Lesson: Signal quality must outweigh volume; a single, well‑structured alert can be more actionable than thousands of noisy ones.  
