INSERT INTO drafts ("id", "window_id", "cluster_id", "channel", "prompt_version", "content", "content_hash", "source_event_ids", "status", "reviewed_by", "review_notes", "approved_at", "created_at", "updated_at", "guardrail_score", "guardrail_passed", "guardrail_reasons", "llm_model", "reviewer_id", "reviewer_note", "reviewed_at", "generated_at") VALUES ('277cf57ffafe579c87908cd02df23f34'::uuid, '20260623T1459_20261005T1859', '20260623T1459_20261005T1859_c1', 'LINKEDIN', 'v2', 'SSH reverse tunnels can expose a local service to the internet without a public IP. A recent Hacker News discussion highlighted a self‑hosted HTTP tunnel using SSH and Nginx, garnering 165 points and 34 comments. The solution shows how ssh -R can forward a local port to a remote server, turning a private machine into a reachable endpoint. This method bypasses the need for a VPN or router configuration. The approach uses port 443 on the remote side to accept HTTPS requests, then forwards them to the local service over SSH (https://vincent.bernat.ch/en/blog/2026-http-over-ssh).

- The tunnel opens a listening port on the remote server and forwards traffic back to the local machine via SSH, keeping the connection encrypted.  
- Using Nginx as a reverse proxy on the remote host allows HTTP traffic to be routed to the forwarded port, enabling webhooks and API access.  
- Tradeoff: the remote server must allow GatewayPorts and the local machine must keep the SSH session alive; otherwise, the tunnel closes.  

SSH reverse tunnels provide a lightweight, secure alternative to VPNs for exposing services behind NAT. How do you decide when to use an SSH tunnel versus a VPN in your infrastructure?', '17c04d14944cb1aa29cf83931301798d21634eb4e7a81858634ae59178e953b7', '{9a5d100a21e947dab78a90eed77b992e,d45ac8deb03b4713a75c61abd5fecd9d,cd7dc6fe62424bdebc2827c107bf7bd5,754d5805d98f4efb892a48192a51f237}'::uuid[]::uuid[], 'IN_REVIEW', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2026-10-05 18:52:32.117938+00:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-05 19:00:10.798644+00:00'::timestamptz, 1.0, true, '{}'::jsonb, 'openai/gpt-oss-20b', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2026-10-05 19:00:10.784962+00:00'::timestamptz) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO drafts ("id", "window_id", "cluster_id", "channel", "prompt_version", "content", "content_hash", "source_event_ids", "status", "reviewed_by", "review_notes", "approved_at", "created_at", "updated_at", "guardrail_score", "guardrail_passed", "guardrail_reasons", "llm_model", "reviewer_id", "reviewer_note", "reviewed_at", "generated_at") VALUES ('9f4f0185c72c5558af2b824081623a0a'::uuid, '20260623T1607_20261005T2007', '20260623T1607_20261005T2007_c0', 'LINKEDIN', 'v2', 'AI governance starts with a clear inventory.  
During the first four weeks of any AI program, most companies report only licensed tools and seat counts, overlooking scripts, marketing assistants, and vendor‑triggered updates that actually run AI models. At a recent summit in Barcelona, 30 % of participants admitted they had no documented authority to stop an AI system they oversee.  

What changed:  
- A four‑step process was adopted: identify every AI system, classify it by risk level, list the specific risks, and assign a mitigation owner.  
- Responsibility is now attached to an individual who can halt the system, not just a checkbox in a policy.  
- The “human in the loop” label was clarified—authority is defined by the documented mitigation owner, not by vague procedural language.  

Lesson: Governance succeeds when stopping power is tied to a named person with clear authority, not merely to a process.  

