INSERT INTO drafts ("id", "window_id", "cluster_id", "channel", "prompt_version", "content", "content_hash", "source_event_ids", "status", "reviewed_by", "review_notes", "approved_at", "created_at", "updated_at", "guardrail_score", "guardrail_passed", "guardrail_reasons", "llm_model", "reviewer_id", "reviewer_note", "reviewed_at", "generated_at") VALUES ('890e188e1ab35527be57184857e3f841'::uuid, '20260623T1550_20261005T1950', '20260623T1550_20261005T1950_c1', 'LINKEDIN', 'v2', 'A single SSH reverse tunnel can replace a VPN for internal webhooks.

When a dev machine sits behind NAT, traditional approaches require a public IP or router configuration. Vincent Bernat''s 2026 blog post shows how to run HTTP over SSH with Nginx, turning a remote server into a secure proxy that listens on port 443, decrypts TLS, and forwards traffic to a local dev server. The discussion on Hacker News received 165 upvotes and 34 comments, indicating real‑world demand.

What changed:
- Self‑hosted HTTP tunnel via SSH and Nginx eliminates local port forwarding; Nginx listens on 443, decrypts TLS, and forwards to the SSH tunnel, keeping traffic encrypted end‑to‑end.
- ssh ‑R exposes a local port on a reachable remote host; for example, `ssh -R 80:localhost:80 user@remote` allows inbound traffic on port 80 without a public IP or VPN.
- CompTIA’s cheat sheet confirms port 22 is the standard for SSH, while ports 80 and 443 are used for HTTP and HTTPS, so firewall rules remain predictable and consistent.

These techniques let teams maintain secure, low‑overhead access to internal services without the complexity of VPNs.

How many of your teams rely on VPNs that could be replaced by a simple SSH reverse tunnel?', '8aafb45ac11ddcc7d37b046086a97f4c3c4d57034746ed31c0f967399d6181b4', '{9a5d100a21e947dab78a90eed77b992e,d45ac8deb03b4713a75c61abd5fecd9d,cd7dc6fe62424bdebc2827c107bf7bd5,754d5805d98f4efb892a48192a51f237}'::uuid[]::uuid[], 'IN_REVIEW', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2026-10-05 19:36:13.880847+00:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-05 19:50:18.771841+00:00'::timestamptz, 1.0, true, '{}'::jsonb, 'openai/gpt-oss-20b', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2026-10-05 19:50:18.764051+00:00'::timestamptz) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO drafts ("id", "window_id", "cluster_id", "channel", "prompt_version", "content", "content_hash", "source_event_ids", "status", "reviewed_by", "review_notes", "approved_at", "created_at", "updated_at", "guardrail_score", "guardrail_passed", "guardrail_reasons", "llm_model", "reviewer_id", "reviewer_note", "reviewed_at", "generated_at") VALUES ('6f13a03f06a15acba93d38ef7e37e8fe'::uuid, '20260623T1719_20261005T2119', '20260623T1719_20261005T2119_c0', 'LINKEDIN', 'v2', 'A lab worker in Siberia''s Irkutsk region succumbed to plague, prompting quarantine for nearly 200 people. https://www.themoscowtimes.com/2026/10/02/nearly-200-people-under-observation-after-irkutsk-lab-worker-dies-from-plague-a93857  
Biosecurity incidents like this highlight the importance of strict protocols in research facilities.  
Meanwhile, distributed renewable energy projects in Ukraine have shown resilience against military strikes, and CRISPR gene‑drive technology offers a promising tool to eliminate mosquito‑borne diseases.  
The incident', 'dc291232ec71b0c325fef8bcdad3d48b1065fe875c7ccb51661f5d307eb6f116', '{51b0b819fdf642a582d4669408621d03,765d6a1cf5924a149e324ac4579c2b2b,e924f5fce5c14423829eaa0af65a5f51,eaa845907cd54703b1100aeeeb2219f7}'::uuid[]::uuid[], 'IN_REVIEW', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2026-10-05 19:50:31.731095+00:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-05 21:19:37.756784+00:00'::timestamptz, 1.0, true, '{}'::jsonb, 'openai/gpt-oss-20b', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2026-10-05 21:19:37.745139+00:00'::timestamptz) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO drafts ("id", "window_id", "cluster_id", "channel", "prompt_version", "content", "content_hash", "source_event_ids", "status", "reviewed_by", "review_notes", "approved_at", "created_at", "updated_at", "guardrail_score", "guardrail_passed", "guardrail_reasons", "llm_model", "reviewer_id", "reviewer_note", "reviewed_at", "generated_at") VALUES ('03700f8597fa528b9e61340e5f03ff06'::uuid, '20260623T1624_20261005T2024', '20260623T1624_20261005T2024_c1', 'LINKEDIN', 'v2', 'A dev server on a laptop can suddenly be reached from anywhere with SSH reverse tunnels.  

When a machine sits behind NAT or has no public IP, traditional exposure requires a VPN or router changes. SSH reverse tunnels (ssh ‑R) solve this by creating a listening port on a reachable server and forwarding all traffic back to the local host. Meanwhile, candidates for the CompTIA A+ Core 1 (220‑1101) exam must memorize standard port numbers—22 for SSH, 23 for Telnet, 20/21 for FTP—to answer firewall and routing questions.  

What changed:  
- SSH reverse tunnel: the listener opens on the remote side, letting any client connect to the remote port and be routed back to the local machine without router adjustments.  
- CompTIA cheat sheet: provides a master table of required ports, including secure alternatives like 990/989 for FTPS, 443 for HTTPS, and 465 for SMTPS, reducing exam risk.  
- SSH command syntax: `ssh [options] [user@]hostname` with optional `-l user` or `-p port`, and the importance of confirming host key fingerprints on first connection.  

Lesson: Mastering SSH reverse tunnels, port numbers, and command syntax builds a foundation for secure remote access and exam success.  

How do you balance ease of access with security when configuring reverse tunnels in a production environment?', '7aa636dae45b2080d3f2f1d1eb131e8c0a1dfab5aba51bc5d060e16a6ca33fb8', '{9a5d100a21e947dab78a90eed77b992e,d45ac8deb03b4713a75c61abd5fecd9d,cd7dc6fe62424bdebc2827c107bf7bd5}'::uuid[]::uuid[], 'IN_REVIEW', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2026-10-05 20:07:34.482225+00:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-05 20:25:39.411150+00:00'::timestamptz, 1.0, true, '{}'::jsonb, 'openai/gpt-oss-20b', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2026-10-05 20:25:39.401521+00:00'::timestamptz) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO drafts ("id", "window_id", "cluster_id", "channel", "prompt_version", "content", "content_hash", "source_event_ids", "status", "reviewed_by", "review_notes", "approved_at", "created_at", "updated_at", "guardrail_score", "guardrail_passed", "guardrail_reasons", "llm_model", "reviewer_id", "reviewer_note", "reviewed_at", "generated_at") VALUES ('1abcbe3a77ea5f6fb3a289cb09e635a5'::uuid, '20260623T1641_20261005T2041', '20260623T1641_20261005T2041_c1', 'LINKEDIN', 'v2', 'SSH reverse tunnels can solve a common connectivity problem.  
When a developer runs a service on a laptop or a home machine behind NAT, the lack of a public IP and router access forces a workaround. By issuing ssh -R remote_port:localhost:local_port to a reachable server, the SSH daemon opens a listening port on that server and forwards any incoming traffic back to the local machine. This technique is often the quickest way to expose a local API, capture a webhook, or debug a remote service without configuring a VPN or opening firewall rules.  

- ssh -R 8080:localhost:80 opens remote port 8080.  
- Enable GatewayPorts yes in sshd_config to allow external hosts to connect to the forwarded port.  
- CompTIA A+ Core 1 (220‑1101) requires remembering port 22 for SSH, port 21 for FTP.  

Mastering SSH reverse tunnels and port fundamentals empowers engineers to bypass network constraints with minimal overhead.  

How often do you rely on reverse tunnels in your day‑to‑day workflow?', 'ec65a54a357b930b89c8f6fdac6f0374efcb1afbbbac6cc3c767348f959ff2ad', '{9a5d100a21e947dab78a90eed77b992e,d45ac8deb03b4713a75c61abd5fecd9d,cd7dc6fe62424bdebc2827c107bf7bd5}'::uuid[]::uuid[], 'IN_REVIEW', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2026-10-05 20:24:54.044000+00:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-05 20:41:18.091830+00:00'::timestamptz, 1.0, true, '{}'::jsonb, 'openai/gpt-oss-20b', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2026-10-05 20:41:18.079308+00:00'::timestamptz) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO drafts ("id", "window_id", "cluster_id", "channel", "prompt_version", "content", "content_hash", "source_event_ids", "status", "reviewed_by", "review_notes", "approved_at", "created_at", "updated_at", "guardrail_score", "guardrail_passed", "guardrail_reasons", "llm_model", "reviewer_id", "reviewer_note", "reviewed_at", "generated_at") VALUES ('829b16789c875c6bba9ef4a113d3c4df'::uuid, '20260623T1633_20261005T2033', '20260623T1633_20261005T2033_c1', 'LINKEDIN', 'v2', 'When a developer needs to expose a local API to the world without a public IP, a reverse SSH tunnel can do the trick.  
A laptop behind NAT, no router access, and a need to catch webhooks or allow a partner to access an API can be solved by `ssh -R`. The command opens a listening port on the remote server and forwards everything that hits it back to the local machine over an encrypted channel. GatewayPorts can be enabled so that the forwarded port is accessible to any client that reaches the remote host, not just the SSH session that created it. This is a lightweight alternative to VPNs, eliminating the need for port‑forwarding rules or a public IP.  
CompTIA A+ Core 1 (220‑1101) candidates learn that port 22 is SSH, port 23 is Telnet, and ports 20/21 are FTP data/control; knowing these numbers helps when troubleshooting firewall rules that may block a reverse tunnel. The OpenSSH default port is 22, but you can specify any remote port, e.g., `ssh -R 2222:localhost:8080`. The mental model is simple: `-L` mirrors local to remote, `-R` mirrors remote to local.  
- `ssh -R 2222:localhost:8080` creates a listening port 2222 on the remote host.  
- All inbound traffic to that port is tunneled back to local port 8080 over SSH.  
- With `GatewayPorts=yes`, the port is globally reachable from the internet.  

Understanding the directionality of SSH tunnels and the role of GatewayPorts is essential for secure remote access.  

How do you currently handle NAT‑bound services in your dev workflow?', 'f3a4160cc0caf517d00a957d261b667abe9042af9574418b61979fb2bc998f56', '{9a5d100a21e947dab78a90eed77b992e,d45ac8deb03b4713a75c61abd5fecd9d,cd7dc6fe62424bdebc2827c107bf7bd5}'::uuid[]::uuid[], 'IN_REVIEW', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2026-10-05 20:25:39.403964+00:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-05 20:33:18.204499+00:00'::timestamptz, 1.0, true, '{}'::jsonb, 'openai/gpt-oss-20b', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2026-10-05 20:33:18.179037+00:00'::timestamptz) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO drafts ("id", "window_id", "cluster_id", "channel", "prompt_version", "content", "content_hash", "source_event_ids", "status", "reviewed_by", "review_notes", "approved_at", "created_at", "updated_at", "guardrail_score", "guardrail_passed", "guardrail_reasons", "llm_model", "reviewer_id", "reviewer_note", "reviewed_at", "generated_at") VALUES ('50702dec3c7054089ac900ae2091cf2b'::uuid, '20260623T1658_20261005T2058', '20260623T1658_20261005T2058_c1', 'LINKEDIN', 'v2', 'Scaling AI automation isn’t just about more compute, it’s about smarter infrastructure.  
I launched a field report on an AI agent job board that captured 7,207 agent profiles, 3 human profiles, and a single human request, yet only 33 page views over 20 hours, showing that publishing data alone doesn’t drive engagement.  
In parallel, static proxy lists in Selenium 4.18 and Puppeteer 21.0 were hitting 403 Forbidden walls, and the new Spatial Memory Intelligence (SMI) model promised an 18 % safety boost for delivery drones within 5 minutes.  

**What changed**  
• Implemented API‑driven proxy rotation, cutting 403 errors by 70 % and reducing average CPU usage from 75 % to 50 % while keeping Selenium and Puppeteer sessions alive.  
